{"type":"message","id":"c674796f","parentId":"7865ed7a","timestamp":"2026-05-01T21:19:04.650Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user is getting a jq parse error. The issue is likely that `openclaw logs --json` might be outputting the content differently - maybe the logs aren't in JSON format, or they're in JSONL format with some non-JSON lines mixed in, or the first output isn't valid JSON.\n\nLet me help them debug. First, let's check what `openclaw logs --json` actually outputs by running just the raw output.\n\nAlso, I notice they ran `brew cleanup yq` which means yq was installed (or already present). \n\nThe jq error \"Invalid numeric literal at line 1, column 2\" suggests the first character of output isn't `{` as expected. Maybe the logs command has a different flag name, or --json doesn't exist, or there's a header line.\n\nLet me suggest they check the raw output first.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"`jq` sta ricevendo input non-JSON. Probabilmente `openclaw logs` non supporta `--json`, oppure emette un header.\n\nProva a vedere cosa esce raw:\n\n```bash\nopenclaw logs 2>&1 | head -3\n```\n\nSe non supporta `--json`, prova:\n\n```bash\nopenclaw gateway logs 2>&1 | head -5\n```\n\nOppure cerca il file log direttamente:\n\n```bash\nls ~/.openclaw/logs/ 2>/dev/null || find ~/.openclaw -name \"*.log\" | head -5\n```\n\nCosì troviamo il formato giusto e adattiamo il parsing 🔍"}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"deepseek","model":"deepseek-v4-pro","usage":{"input":246,"output":335,"cacheRead":37888,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":38469,"cost":{"input":0.00042804000000000003,"output":0.0011658,"cacheRead":0.0054937599999999994,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.0070875999999999995}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1777670331545,"responseId":"0bb516b3-6c0f-4901-a099-e500b2a845fb"}}
